 "nested": {
  "question": "Independently calculate this finite case: on Z/11Z with unit cycle edges, what are the shortest-path distances from 0 of the image of unit displacement 1 under multiplication by 3 and under its multiplicative inverse? Is the claim that both distances are at most 3 true? Provide the calculation.",
  "parent": "claude (headless CLI)",
  "child_model": "Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct GGUF Q4_0",
  "parent_chose_to_delegate": false,
  "child": null,
  "parent_answer": "**Calculation** (done by hand, no tools run)\n\nThe graph is the 11-cycle on Z/11Z with edges between x and x\u00b11, so the shortest-path distance from 0 is d(0, x) = min(x mod 11, 11 \u2212 (x mod 11)).\n\n- **Multiplication by 3:** 3 \u00b7 1 = 3, and d(0, 3) = min(3, 8) = **3**.\n- **Multiplicative inverse of 3:** 3 \u00b7 4 = 12 \u2261 1 (mod 11), so 3\u207b\u00b9 = 4. Then 4 \u00b7 1 = 4, and d(0, 4) = min(4, 7) = **4**.\n\n**Verdict:** the claim that both distances are at most 3 is **false (refuted)**. The image under multiplication by 3 is at distance 3, but the image under the inverse is at distance 4, which exceeds 3.\n\nI did not consult the reviewer, so there is no receipt ID to quote.",
